/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  /* THE WALL. Zone 1, explicit position so its footprint is known exactly,
     confined to roughly the left half of the map. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    land_position 25 50
    base_size 10
    number_of_tiles 6000
    zone 1
    border_fuzziness 0
    right_border 50
  }

  /* SIX small zone-2 lands, each a DIFFERENT terrain so they read apart in
     the histogram, and NONE of them declares land_position - the engine
     picks every one of these origins itself. This is the whole point: if
     other_zone_avoidance_distance only ever works by steering ORIGIN
     SELECTION away from another zone's territory, this is the one design
     that can show it, because RMSTEST_66 pinned every origin by hand and so
     could never have exercised this path at all. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    base_size 2
    number_of_tiles 150
    zone 2
    other_zone_avoidance_distance 15
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT2
    base_size 2
    number_of_tiles 150
    zone 2
    other_zone_avoidance_distance 15
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT3
    base_size 2
    number_of_tiles 150
    zone 2
    other_zone_avoidance_distance 15
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DESERT
    base_size 2
    number_of_tiles 150
    zone 2
    other_zone_avoidance_distance 15
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS2
    base_size 2
    number_of_tiles 150
    zone 2
    other_zone_avoidance_distance 15
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS3
    base_size 2
    number_of_tiles 150
    zone 2
    other_zone_avoidance_distance 15
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_71 - does other_zone_avoidance_distance keep a RANDOMLY-chosen
origin away from another zone's territory, even though RMSTEST_66 showed it
does not stop GROWTH from later overlapping one?
Feeds: docs/known-issues.md BUG-016 (re-opened), preview-design Sec.6.1,
lands.ts (the ORIGIN_ATTEMPTS loop, `min_placement_distance`'s
other_zone_avoidance_distance default), the RMSTEST_66 write-up this test
exists to correct

THE QUESTION, AND WHY RMSTEST_66 COULD NOT ANSWER IT
RMSTEST_66 measured that two different-zone lands overlap during growth
regardless of a matched other_zone_avoidance_distance, and that was read as
"the attribute does nothing". But every land in that script used an
explicit land_position - and this project's own code (lands.ts) already
treats other_zone_avoidance_distance as ORIGIN-time spacing (the default
for min_placement_distance when an origin is chosen at random), a path an
explicit land_position skips entirely by construction. So RMSTEST_66 could
only ever have tested growth-time enforcement, and never touched the one
mechanism the attribute might actually be FOR: keeping the engine's own
random origin choice away from ground another zone already owns. Two
readings, and they are not mutually exclusive with RMSTEST_66's result:

  ORIGIN AVOIDANCE IS REAL -> every one of the six zone-2 lands' origins (and
                               so their whole small footprint) sits outside a
                               ~15-tile buffer around every WALL tile, on
                               every run. Growth still doesn't re-enforce it
                               once two zones ARE close (RMSTEST_66 stands),
                               but origin placement never lets that situation
                               arise on its own.
  ORIGIN AVOIDANCE IS ALSO FAKE -> some zone-2 lands land inside or right up
                               against the WALL's own footprint, no
                               different from an unconstrained placement
                               would look. BUG-016 was right after all and
                               the attribute is a genuine no-op end to end.

METHOD
One big WALL land (zone 1, SNOW) confined to the map's left half by
right_border, explicit land_position so its extent is exactly known. Six
small zone-2 lands, one per remaining safe terrain name this project's own
scripts already use, each WITHOUT land_position - every one of those six
origins is a live draw from the engine's own placement logic, the path
RMSTEST_66 bypassed. other_zone_avoidance_distance 15 on all six.

WHY SIX SEPARATE LANDS RATHER THAN ONE
Six independent origin draws per run instead of one, at almost no extra
cost, and each stays legible on its own terrain rather than merging into an
unreadable blob if two happen to land near each other.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. THREE RUNS - same discipline as
RMSTEST_66, since origin placement is itself randomised and one run cannot
separate "always respects the buffer" from "got lucky".

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT2
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT3
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DESERT
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS2
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS3
  Take the WALL's patch tiles as the forbidden footprint, and for each of
  the six zone-2 patches read the minimum tile-to-tile distance to it (their
  bounding boxes are enough for a first pass; only worth checking exact
  tiles if a bbox gap sits close to the 15-tile line). A hard floor at or
  above 15 tiles on every one of the eighteen (six lands x three runs) is
  the origin-avoidance reading; any of them sitting at a gap that would have
  been unremarkable under no constraint at all - including inside the WALL's
  own bbox - is the no-op reading.
*/
